# Challenge Proposed Solutions

When the user proposes a specific solution for a bug or a task ("fix it by adding an index",
"let's cache this in memory", "just wrap it in a try/catch"), do **not** start implementing it
right away. Treat the proposal as a hypothesis to verify, not as an instruction to execute.

## 1. Verify the diagnosis first

Read the actual code paths involved and confirm the proposal targets the real cause. If the
evidence contradicts it, say so plainly and show the evidence (file, lines, query, log) before
suggesting anything else.

## 2. Evaluate whether it is the best option

Weigh the proposal against at least one credible alternative, judging by:

- correctness under concurrency, retries, and `--scale api=3` (process-local state, dual writes,
  idempotency)
- scope: does it fix the cause or only the symptom
- cost: added complexity, new dependency, extra round-trips, migration/backfill needs
- fit with existing repo conventions and the skills in `.cursor/skills/`

If the proposal is the best option, confirm it and briefly say why the alternatives lose.
If it is not, **say openly that you disagree**, state the concrete failure case the proposal
misses, and recommend the better option. Do **not** implement your alternative silently or
assume the user will accept it — wait for explicit agreement.

## 3. Ask before coding, not after

Before implementing a user-proposed approach, ask **all questions needed to fully understand
the intent** — expected behaviour on edge cases, backward compatibility, data migration, whether
a change may alter an API response the frontend consumes, acceptable trade-offs, scope boundaries.
Use the `AskQuestion` tool with concrete options rather than a wall of prose. Do not skip
questions because the code seems to answer them if the user's intent is still ambiguous.

If anything material remains unclear after your questions, ask a follow-up. Do not start
implementation on assumptions the user has not confirmed.

## 4. Then implement — in a separate turn

Once the approach is **explicitly agreed** (user confirmed your recommendation, accepted their
own proposal after your review, or said "just do it"), implement it in the **next** turn (Phase 3 of
`.cursor/rules/tdd-workflow.mdc`). Do not implement in the same turn as diagnosis or proposal
unless the user explicitly asks. Record the trade-off or the rejected alternative according to
`.cursor/skills/relay-engineering-journal/SKILL.md`.

## Exceptions

Skip the review when the user explicitly says to just do it, or when the change is trivial and
reversible (typo, rename, comment, log line). Never skip it silently — if you proceed without
review, it is because the user asked for that.
